--- /dev/null
+++ b/debian/patches/0099-add-dolby-vision-rpu-passthrough-to-hevc-vaapi-encoder.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,707 @@
+Add Dolby Vision RPU passthrough to the HEVC VAAPI encoder.
+
+hevc_vaapi drops Dolby Vision on transcode. FFmpeg has all the machinery --
+the HEVC decoder parses the RPU and attaches AV_FRAME_DATA_DOVI_METADATA to
+every frame, and dovi_rpuenc.c can synthesise the RPU back out -- but only
+libx265, libsvtav1 and libaomenc are wired to it. No VAAPI, Vulkan or AMF
+encoder calls ff_dovi_configure(), so on AMD Linux there is no way to keep DV
+through a hardware encode: the current answer is to encode the base layer,
+then re-inject the RPU afterwards with dovi_tool and remux. That matters more
+now that AMD has dropped AMF from the Linux driver (25.20 release notes) and
+told users to move to VA-API, which takes rigaya's VCEEncC -- the one tool
+that does this in a single pass -- off the table.
+
+This wires hevc_vaapi to the existing DOVI code, following libx265.c.
+
+Two things are different from a software encoder and drive the shape of it.
+
+1. The RPU is a SUFFIX NAL. It must be the last NAL of the access unit --
+   hevcdec.c says so in as many words ("RPUs are appended to the end of an AU;
+   they are the last non-EOB/EOS NAL in the AU") and reads
+   s->pkt.nals[s->pkt.nb_nals - 1], and the dovi_rpu BSF likewise only looks at
+   au->units[au->nb_units - 1]. VAAPI packed headers are always written ahead
+   of the slice data, so write_extra_header() cannot place it. The RPU is
+   therefore generated when the picture is issued, carried on the picture, and
+   concatenated onto the coded data in vaapi_encode_get_coded_data().
+
+   Generating at issue time also means the RPUs are produced in encode order,
+   which is bitstream order, which is the order the decoder consumes them in.
+   That is the correct order for a stateful RPU sequence; libx265 feeds them in
+   display order and lets the encoder reorder.
+
+   ff_dovi_rpu_generate() with FF_DOVI_WRAP_NAL returns the emulation-prevented
+   payload beginning with the 0x19 prefix byte and nothing else, so the start
+   code and the two-byte NAL header are prepended here: 0x7c 0x01 is
+   nal_unit_type 62 (UNSPEC62), nuh_layer_id 0, nuh_temporal_id_plus1 1, which
+   is what hevcdec.c requires (!nuh_layer_id && !temporal_id). 0x7e would be
+   type 63, the enhancement layer.
+
+2. ff_dovi_configure() reads avctx->pix_fmt to decide whether the coded samples
+   are 10-bit 4:2:0. For a VAAPI encoder that is AV_PIX_FMT_VAAPI, which tells
+   it nothing, so unless -strict unofficial is passed the profile check zeroes
+   dv_profile and Dolby Vision silently never engages. The configure step here
+   therefore calls ff_dovi_configure_from_codedpar() with the hardware frames'
+   sw_format instead, normalised to AV_PIX_FMT_YUV420P10 when it describes
+   10-bit 4:2:0 -- VAAPI's spelling is P010, which is the same sample format.
+   Anything that is not 10-bit 4:2:0 is passed through unchanged and correctly
+   ends up with no DV profile.
+
+   Going through ff_dovi_configure_from_codedpar() also keeps the configuration
+   record landing on avctx->coded_side_data, so movenc writes dvcC/dvvC (with
+   -strict unofficial) and matroskaenc writes the BlockAdditionMapping. The
+   output is signalled DV without a separate mkvmerge pass.
+
+Behaviour matches libx265: -dolbyvision defaults to auto, meaning DV is enabled
+only when avctx->decoded_side_data carries an AVDOVIMetadata; a frame arriving
+without AV_FRAME_DATA_DOVI_METADATA once a profile is active is an error rather
+than a silently non-conforming stream.
+
+-dolbyvision 1 is required in practice. The auto default never engages under
+the ffmpeg CLI, because avctx->decoded_side_data is not populated with an
+AVDOVIMetadata here -- measured, and libx265 behaves identically on the same
+input, so this is FFmpeg's plumbing rather than anything specific to VAAPI. The
+encoder now says so: if a frame carries DV metadata while no profile is active,
+it warns once that Dolby Vision is being dropped and that -dolbyvision 1 enables
+it.
+
+Profiles 4 and 7 (enhancement layer)
+------------------------------------
+
+dovi_configure_ext() nominally rejects profiles 4 and 7, but that rejection is
+unreachable from here. It keys on the RPU header out of decoded_side_data, and
+with no metadata the profile is instead guessed from the colour tags -- BT.2020
+PQ gives profile 8. A profile 7 source therefore used to produce a stream tagged
+8.1 whose RPUs were still profile 7, describing an enhancement layer the VAAPI
+decoder had already discarded. Nothing complained: the configuration record was
+correct, every frame carried an RPU, and only reading the RPU internals showed
+the mismatch.
+
+So the RPU is brought to the single-layer profile 8 shape here instead, in
+vaapi_encode_h265_dovi_fixup(), on the copy the L5 handling already makes:
+el_spatial_resampling_filter_flag = 0, disable_residual_flag = 1, and
+nlq_method_idc = AV_DOVI_NLQ_NONE. All three are needed -- NLQ is emitted
+whenever nlq_method_idc != NONE, gated independently of disable_residual_flag,
+so clearing only the header flags would write NLQ into a stream that declares no
+residual. The conversion is logged once; it is lossy in principle, though the
+enhancement layer is gone before the encoder ever sees the frame.
+
+Profile 5 is untouched: it has vdr_rpu_profile 0, so the guess returns 5 and the
+conversion does not fire.
+
+As a backstop, the generated RPU's profile is compared against the configuration
+record before it is written, and a mismatch is an error rather than a silently
+mislabelled stream.
+
+Level 5 handling (-dv_l5)
+-------------------------
+
+L5 states the active picture area as four pixel offsets from the frame edges,
+so the display can exclude letterbox bars from its analysis. The offsets are
+absolute pixels: a 2.39:1 film coded 3840x2160 carries top/bottom offsets of
+276 -- measured, not derived. ff_dovi_rpu_generate() writes them through
+verbatim (dovi_rpuenc.c, 13-bit fields) and has no idea what resolution it is
+encoding at, so resizing leaves them stale -- scale to 1920x1080 and the RPU
+still claims 276 rows of bar where there are now 138.
+
+The encoder cannot detect this on its own: the ffmpeg filter chain runs before
+the encoder, so the AVFrame arriving at init_picture_params has already been
+scaled and its dimensions always equal avctx->width/height. The source size
+therefore has to be supplied.
+
+  -dv_l5 keep     (default) pass the offsets through unchanged
+  -dv_l5 zero     clear them, for when the bars were cropped away
+  -dv_l5 scale    rescale them from -dv_l5_canvas to the output size
+
+  -dv_l5_canvas WxH   the frame size the source offsets refer to
+
+So a 2160p source letterboxed in a 16:9 frame, scaled to 1080p, wants
+`-dv_l5 scale -dv_l5_canvas 3840x2160`; a chain that crops the bars off first
+wants `-dv_l5 zero`. The default is keep, so same-resolution encodes are
+unaffected and the option only engages when asked for. dv_l5=scale without
+dv_l5_canvas is rejected at init rather than mid-encode.
+
+Because keep is the default and stale offsets are not detectable from inside the
+encoder -- it never sees the source size, which is the whole reason scale needs a
+canvas -- a source carrying non-zero L5 under keep warns once, naming the two
+options. That fires on correct same-resolution encodes too. There is no better
+test: offsets that are stale after a downscale remain perfectly legal for the
+frame (276+276 fits inside 1080), so a spurious line is the lesser cost.
+
+Left and right scale by the width ratio, top and bottom by the height ratio,
+so a change of aspect is handled. Values round to nearest and clamp to the
+13-bit field. Streams with no L5 block need no adjustment and are left alone;
+the metadata is only copied when there is an L5 block to change, and the copy
+is a byte-for-byte duplicate of the side data (AVDOVIMetadata is a flat buffer
+addressed by internal offsets, so every accessor stays valid).
+
+Not covered:
+
+- Only hevc_vaapi. av1_vaapi would need the T.35 wrapping path
+  (FF_DOVI_WRAP_T35) instead, and h264_vaapi has no DV profile worth having.
+- The non_independent_frame path in vaapi_encode_output(), which concatenates a
+  frame's data into the next packet, is not handled; it is AV1-only, so an HEVC
+  picture never takes it. The RPU is freed with the picture either way.
+- avctx->framerate is used for the DV level calculation, as in libx265, so an
+  unset framerate gives a level derived from 0 fps. Pass -r if it matters.
+
+Verified: builds clean against jellyfin-ffmpeg v8.1.2-2 with the full series
+applied, no new warnings under FFmpeg's own -Wall set, and -h encoder=hevc_vaapi
+lists all three options with the right types and defaults. The L5 functions were
+additionally unit-tested by extracting them verbatim and running them against
+metadata built with av_dovi_metadata_alloc(): keep/zero/scale behaviour, the
+2160->1080 and 2160->720 ratios, independent x and y scaling, rounding, the
+13-bit clamp, the no-L5 and identity no-ops, that the source metadata is never
+mutated, and that neighbouring extension blocks survive the copy intact.
+
+Verified on hardware 2026-08-18: RX 9070 XT, Mesa 26.0.4 (radeonsi, gfx1201),
+jellyfin-ffmpeg v8.1.2-2 with the full series. Per case, checking the output RPU
+with dovi_tool rather than trusting the configuration record -- the record is a
+header claim a stream can carry with no RPU behind it, which is exactly how the
+profile 7 bug hid:
+
+  P8.1  -dolbyvision 1   profile 8 tag, RPU profile 8, 20/20 frames carry an RPU
+  P5    -dolbyvision 1   profile 5 tag, compat 0, not converted
+  P7    -dolbyvision 1   converted; RPU profile 8, disable_residual_flag true,
+                         NLQ absent  (before this change: RPU still profile 7)
+  auto  (any source)     no DV, now warns once
+  L5    keep  4K->1080p  offsets stay 276, now warns once
+  L5    scale + canvas   offsets rescale 276 -> 138
+  mp4   -strict unofficial   dvcC written, hvc1 tag
+
+Two things remain unverified, both for lack of a way to reach them:
+
+- B-frames. This encoder reports "supported references: 1 / 0" on RDNA4/Mesa
+  26.0.4 and produces I and P frames only, so encode order equals display order
+  and nothing exercises the reordering argument above. If a later Mesa enables
+  HEVC B-frames, that argument needs testing rather than assuming.
+- A Dolby Vision display. Everything above is bitstream-level.
+
+Repo-facing summary -- options, status, gotchas, and the gate declaration:
+docs/patches/0004-dolby-vision-hevc-vaapi.md in the build-recipe repo.
